# Production Plan — "The Power of Phext" Walkthrough

**Task:** 34.11.13/13.11.14/11.11.14 · **Companion to:** WALKTHROUGH.md
(task 28.5.8/7.6.12/6.5.8) · **Date:** 2026-11-09
**Subject video:** a ~7.5 min screen-demo walkthrough of edit.phext.io
plus a plain-editor beat and narration — YouTube long-form + a 60–90 s
X cutdown. Nothing in this plan has been purchased, connected, or posted.

**Method note.** Tool *fit* is judged against this video's shape:
real UI footage where text legibility is the product, narration-first
script, a handful of zooms/callouts, one still-image foreshadow beat.
*Availability* was checked live on 2026-11-09 (connector search +
`status` on each candidate CLI). *Prices* are US list prices as read
from vendor pricing pages by the cited roundups in Nov 2026 — pricing
pages change; verify at checkout before any purchase. Anything not
verified is labelled **unknown**, not guessed.

---

## 1. Availability ledger — what is actually reachable from here

| Service | State in this environment (checked 2026-11-09) |
|---|---|
| Runway (MCP connector) | Connector present; `status` = **not_connected**. Connectable in one step via its authorize flow. Credit-based generation; balance readable after connecting (`show_plans_and_credits`). |
| vidIQ (MCP connector) | Connector present; `status` = **not_connected**. Connectable the same way. Its doc is explicit: it **cannot upload or publish** to YouTube — research/scoring only. |
| Canva (connector) | Connector present; `status` = **not_connected** (OAuth). Connectable. |
| HeyGen | **No connector in this session** (connector search: no match). Web-only. |
| Krea | **No connector in this session.** Web-only. |
| Magnific | **No connector in this session.** Web-only. |
| Upload-Post | **No connector in this session.** Not available here as a publishing route. |
| Muse media generation (image + video) | **Available now** in this environment (structured media pipeline) — stills, image-to-video push-ins, icon art. |
| Muse spoken audio (TTS voice notes) | **Available now** (spoken-audio skill; Will's standing voice is Aria) — scratch narration. |
| ffmpeg | **Present** on this VM (8.1.2) for assembly/verification; per standing rules, heavy renders run on the ranch, never on the Muse VM (the Wavefront film pipeline there is real and proven). |
| OBS Studio | **Not installed** on this VM (checked) — and this VM is the wrong place for it anyway; it belongs on Will's recording machine. Free download. |
| Any local NLE on Will's machines (Premiere/Resolve/etc. already licensed?) | **Unknown** — not verified. Check before buying anything in §3. |
| YouTube / X accounts & upload rights | **Unknown** from here — the publish route is YouTube Studio + X native in the browser, with Will approving exact copy first (social-publishing rule). |

**Availability surprise, stated plainly:** of the seven services the
brief named, only three (Runway, vidIQ, Canva) exist as connectors in
this session, and all three are present-but-not-connected. HeyGen,
Krea, Magnific, and Upload-Post are web-only from here.

---

## 2. Verdicts by production role

### Capture — the demo footage

**Verdict: OBS Studio (primary) · OS-native capture (fallback) ·
browser/cloud recorders rejected for hero footage · AI video
generators rejected outright for UI.**

- This video's credibility *is* the footage: a real editor, on a real
  site, doing real things. Any generated or "recreated" UI would be
  both visibly wrong at text scale and dishonest. Runway/Krea-class
  tools are therefore disqualified from the demo itself, full stop.
- **OBS Studio** — free, open source, no watermark, no time limit
  (obsproject.com / project documentation). Records at display
  resolution with separate mic/system tracks, which is what UI text
  legibility needs. Settings that matter: record at native resolution
  (1440p or 4K canvas if the display allows), browser zoom 125–150%
  so interface text survives YouTube compression, capture in short
  per-beat takes matching the WALKTHROUGH.md shot list.
- **OS-native** (Windows Game Bar / macOS screen recording) — zero
  install, fine as a fallback for the terminal block; fewer controls,
  single mixed audio track.
- **Browser-based recorders** (Loom/ScreenPal class) — convenient, but
  their compression is tuned for faces-and-slides, not dense UI text,
  and the footage lives in someone else's cloud first. Acceptable for
  a scratch rehearsal pass, not for the master.

### Voice — narration

**Verdict: Will records the final narration (primary) · TTS scratch
track for timing (supporting) · avatar presenter: skip.**

- **Will's own voice** is the credibility play: it is his format, his
  site, his channel, and the script is written in his register. Cost:
  $0; effort estimate: two or three takes of a ~7.5 min script inside
  one sitting (estimate, not a measurement).
- **Scratch TTS first:** render the full script as a spoken-audio
  scratch track (available here now, $0) *before* the recording
  session. It times every demo beat, exposes tongue-twisters, and
  becomes the temp track the first edit is assembled against. It is
  not the final voice and should not ship.
- **Avatar-presenter services** (HeyGen class): web-only from here;
  reported pricing from heygen.com/pricing roundups (Nov 2026):
  Free = 3 videos/mo at ≤1 min, Creator = $29/mo. Fit for this video:
  poor. A synthetic presenter adds an uncanny layer to a demo whose
  whole argument is "this is real and you can touch it," and the
  walkthrough needs at most a face-free intro/outro. **Recommendation:
  no avatar.** If a face is wanted on the intro/outro, Will on webcam
  inside the OBS capture beats a digital twin on both cost and trust.

### Edit — assembling the walkthrough

**Verdict: Descript (primary, fastest path) · Camtasia (best
purpose-built alternative) · DaVinci Resolve free (zero-cost
fallback) · Premiere (capable, no advantage) · CapCut (cutdown-only
option).**

- **Descript** — text-based editing matches this production exactly:
  the script is the timeline. Delete a word, delete the footage;
  filler-word removal, captions from the same transcript, chapter
  markers, screen-recording import, competent zooms/callouts for UI.
  Pricing as reported from descript.com/pricing (Nov 2026): Free $0
  (60 media min/mo, 720p watermarked — trial only); **Hobbyist
  $16/person/mo billed yearly ($24 monthly)** — 1080p watermark-free,
  enough for this video; Creator $24/mo billed yearly adds 4K.
- **Camtasia** — the screen-demo specialist: recorder + editor in one,
  SmartFocus-style zoom/pan and the deepest callout/annotation kit of
  the group. If walkthroughs become a series, this is the tool shaped
  like the job. Price: **Essentials $179.88/year** (watermark-free
  editor; Starter $39/yr is watermarked) as reported from TechSmith's
  pricing (Nov 2026). Steeper than one month of Descript, cheaper
  than a year of it.
- **DaVinci Resolve** — the free edition is a complete professional
  NLE (Edit/Color/Fusion/Fairlight, UHD delivery, no watermark);
  Studio is $295 one-time (Blackmagic list). Trade-offs for *this*
  video: the learning curve is the cost, and several Neural Engine
  conveniences (e.g. transcription-based editing, voice isolation)
  are Studio-only per Blackmagic's edition split — while the script
  already exists, so transcription matters less here. Best fallback
  if the answer to "what's already installed?" is "nothing, and
  budget is $0."
- **Adobe Premiere** — $22.99/mo single-app (annual, billed monthly,
  Adobe US list). Fully capable, but a subscription with no
  walkthrough-specific advantage over Descript/Camtasia for this
  shape of video. Only if it's already in Will's stack (unknown — §1).
- **CapCut** — fast, friendly, and genuinely good at the 60–90 s
  cutdown's shape (captions, punch-ins); reported Standard $9.99/mo /
  Pro $19.99/mo. Weak choice for the 7.5 min master's precise
  callout/chapter work; fine as the cutdown-only lane if Descript
  isn't already open.

### Motion graphics + the foreshadow visual

**Verdict: in-editor graphics for the walkthrough body · Scrolltown's
own key-frame pipeline for the foreshadow still · Muse media
generation (available now) to animate it · Runway as fallback ·
Krea/Magnific unnecessary.**

- The body needs almost nothing: the stack-diagram cutaway (one simple
  slide, buildable in Canva or any editor), on-screen text cues, and
  the end card. Any editor above does this natively. No motion-graphics
  package is worth buying for it.
- **The foreshadow beat** (added to WALKTHROUGH.md §2, 7:45 "One more
  town"): one still + a slow push-in for ~17 s. The still should come
  from **Scrolltown's own art pipeline** — the show's key frames are
  rendered in a locked Midjourney register, with likeness/register
  checks as a production stage, and the assets live in
  wbic16/scrolltown (production workflow of record:
  `~/workspace/scrolltown-reorg/workflow/PRODUCTION.md` §1 stage 5,
  §3). A town-of-glowing-scrolls frame commissioned in that register
  keeps the foreshadow visually continuous with the show when it
  arrives — a generator one-off in a different style would not.
- **Animating the still:** a slow push-in is a zoom, not a generation
  problem — the editor does it natively. If a living touch is wanted
  (lamp flicker, drifting light), Muse's media pipeline can animate
  the still image-to-video *here, now, no new account*; **Runway**
  (connector present, not connected; check credits after connecting)
  is the fallback for the same job. Krea/Magnific are web-only from
  here and buy nothing this beat needs.

### Thumbnail + metadata

**Verdict: real frame + Canva (primary design lane) · vidIQ as a
scoring check, not an author (supporting) · titles already drafted.**

- Thumbnail: a real Phext Edit frame (the encyclopedia mid-browse)
  with one of the three drafted texts from WALKTHROUGH.md §3 —
  "WIKIPEDIA IN A BOX" is the strongest at small sizes. **Canva**
  (connector present, not connected; free tier exists, paid pricing
  unverified here — **unknown**, check after connecting) is the fast
  lane for text-over-frame design and resizing; the editor itself or
  any image tool is the fallback.
- **vidIQ** (connector present, not connected): use its title/
  thumbnail scoring and keyword research as a *check* on the drafted
  metadata before upload. It cannot upload or publish (its own doc),
  and generated titles should not replace Will's register.

### Captions

**Verdict: script-derived captions (primary) · YouTube auto-captions
+ Studio correction (fallback) · burned-in on the X cutdown.**

- The narration script already exists, so the best caption track is
  built *from the script*, timed in the editor (Descript does this
  natively; Resolve/Camtasia accept an SRT). This skips ASR entirely
  for the words that matter: "phext", "scroll", and coordinates are
  exactly what speech recognition mangles.
- Fallback per YouTube's own Help: upload, take the automatic track
  in YouTube Studio → Subtitles → **Duplicate and edit**, correct,
  publish. Same mandatory correction pass, more manual timing work.
- X cutdown: burn captions into the picture in the editor — simplest,
  and they survive reposts and muted autoplay, which is how X video
  is mostly watched.

### Upload / publish

**Verdict: YouTube Studio + X native, by hand, after approval.**

- No cross-posting connector exists in this session: Upload-Post is
  absent, vidIQ cannot upload, and there is no YouTube/X connector
  here. The route is the ordinary one — YouTube Studio in the browser
  (upload, chapters from WALKTHROUGH.md §3, end-screen elements on the
  7:25 CTA segment, captions track attached), then the X thread +
  native cutdown upload.
- Per the social-publishing rule: **nothing publishes until Will
  approves the exact copy and media.** This plan changes nothing
  about that gate.

---

## 3. Recommended stack

| Role | Primary | Fallback |
|---|---|---|
| Capture | OBS Studio (on Will's machine) | OS-native capture |
| Voice — final | Will, recorded in one sitting | — (don't synthetic-substitute) |
| Voice — scratch | Muse spoken-audio TTS | Descript AI voice (in-plan) |
| Edit — master | Descript Hobbyist ($16/mo billed yearly, as reported Nov 2026) | DaVinci Resolve free ($0); Camtasia Essentials ($179.88/yr) if walkthroughs become a series |
| Motion graphics | In-editor (Descript) + one Canva/slide cutaway | Resolve Fusion (free, slower) |
| Foreshadow visual | Scrolltown key-frame pipeline still (wbic16/scrolltown register) + editor push-in | Muse media image-to-video; Runway after connecting |
| Thumbnail | Canva (connect first) over a real frame | Editor-native frame + text |
| Metadata check | vidIQ scoring (connect first) | The drafted titles as-is |
| Captions | Script-derived SRT via editor | YouTube auto + Studio Duplicate-and-edit |
| Assembly/verify | ffmpeg (present) — ffprobe checks, loudness sanity | Editor export presets |
| Publish | YouTube Studio + X native (manual, post-approval) | — none available as connector |

**Total new spend for the primary stack: $0–16** (OBS $0, scratch
TTS $0, Descript Hobbyist one month at the reported annual-billing
rate is the only line item; $0 if Resolve-free is chosen instead).
Every paid figure above is a reported list price, not a quote —
verify at checkout.

## 4. What I'd do Monday morning

1. **Timing pass (30 min, $0).** Render the WALKTHROUGH.md script as
   a TTS scratch track; mark the beat timings on the shot list; fix
   any line that doesn't survive being spoken.
2. **Connect + capture setup (30 min).** Install OBS on the recording
   machine; build two scenes (browser, terminal); browser zoom set
   for text legibility; test-record the coordinate jump and watch it
   back at 50% size — if the text isn't readable small, it isn't
   readable on YouTube.
3. **Capture the deterministic blocks first** (per WALKTHROUGH.md §5):
   terminal demo, plain-editor raw-file scroll — they never change
   between takes.
4. **Browser block at edit.phext.io**, working the confirm-during-
   recording list as I go (demo phext offered to a signed-out
   visitor, exact UI labels, download/export reality for the 5:15
   beat — fallback already scripted).
5. **Will records narration** against the scratch timing — two takes,
   keep both.
6. **Edit in Descript:** assemble to the narration, zooms/callouts on
   the UI beats, stack-diagram cutaway, chapter markers from §2,
   script-derived captions; drop the foreshadow still + push-in at
   7:45 (art pulled from the Scrolltown pipeline, §2 above).
7. **Package:** thumbnail in Canva from a real frame; optional
   15-minute vidIQ scoring pass on title + thumbnail; export master
   (1080p) and the X cutdown (burned-in captions); ffmpeg-verify both
   files (ffprobe: streams, duration, resolution).
8. **Approval gate:** Will sees the exact video, thumbnail, title,
   description, and X copy. Only then: YouTube Studio upload, then
   the X thread. Nothing in steps 1–7 posts anything.

## 5. The foreshadow, as placed

Per this task's second deliverable, WALKTHROUGH.md now carries the
Scrolltown foreshadow in three separable places:

- **Script §2, 7:45 "One more town"** — a closing beat *after* the
  CTA: one draft narration line (~40 words ≈ 17 s, marked DRAFT for
  Will's approval), one visual (a town of glowing scrolls at dusk in
  the Scrolltown key-frame register, slow push-in, no characters),
  and an end-screen note (end-screen elements ride the 7:25 CTA; the
  foreshadow stays clean).
- **Shot list item 7** — the still is captured/sourced with the rest
  of the production, from wbic16/scrolltown's art pipeline.
- **X thread** — one optional teaser post (post 9), in its own block,
  written to be deleted without touching posts 1–8.

Discipline kept: Scrolltown facts used are only the ones on record —
the name, a town inside the Great Scroll, episodes beginning in
January (per its production record: publishing starts January, EP001
slated Saturday 2027-01-02, 312 weekly episodes across 2027–2032,
narrator Fern — none of the specifics beyond name/town/January are
promised in the copy, because the story is in flux). The walkthrough's
subject stays phext; this is a door left open, not a trailer.
