# The Power of Phext — Walkthrough Package

**Framing:** Wikipedia in a box, without a database backend.
**Hero surface:** Phext Edit — https://edit.phext.io
**Supporting artefact:** `demo/` in this folder — a working mini-encyclopedia in one phext file, free to download and run.
**Status:** package for Will's approval. Nothing in here has been posted.

---

## 1. Concept + hook

Every encyclopedia you have ever used is a database wearing a website as
a hat. The articles live in tables; a server you don't control answers
your questions; the knowledge is only yours while the backend is up and
lets you in.

Phext removes the backend. An encyclopedia becomes **one plain-text
file** in which every article lives at an address — a coordinate — and
the addressing does the work the database used to do. You can open the
file, read it, diff it, email it, version it, seal it. To use it you
need a text editor. To *browse* it beautifully, you need Phext Edit.

**The one-sentence pitch:**

> Phext is Wikipedia in a box: an entire encyclopedia as one addressable
> plain-text file — no database backend, no lock-in — and you can try it
> right now, in your browser, at edit.phext.io.

**Site tagline (the site's own words):** "Write in 11 dimensions:
plain text, no lock-in."

---

## 2. YouTube script (target 6–8 minutes)

Narration is written for ~140 words/minute. On-screen cues in
`[brackets]`. Anything marked **[CONFIRM]** is a recording-day check
listed in §5 — the script is worded so it stays true either way.

### Chapter list (upload with the video)

```
0:00 Wikipedia, no database
0:35 The backend problem
1:20 What phext actually is
2:00 Phext Edit: the tour
3:10 Jump to a coordinate
3:50 Search the whole lattice
4:30 Edit one scroll, save
5:15 Download it, open it in Notepad
6:00 Carry your own encyclopedia
6:50 Why this matters (and the AI angle)
7:25 Try it yourself
7:45 One more town (foreshadow)
```

### Script

**0:00 — Cold open.**
`[SCREEN: edit.phext.io already open on a phext, mid-browse. Cursor moves through the lattice.]`
"This is an encyclopedia. Not a website backed by an encyclopedia —
the encyclopedia itself. Every article in it lives at an address, in
one plain-text file, and there is no database anywhere in this picture.
No server farm, no SQL, no backend to keep alive. Just text, with
coordinates. Watch."

**0:35 — The problem.**
`[SCREEN: cutaway — a typical wiki stack diagram: browser → app server → database. Each layer gets a red X in turn.]`
"We're used to knowledge being locked behind backends. Your notes app,
your wiki, your company handbook — the words are yours, but they sit in
somebody's database, in somebody's format, retrievable only while
their server runs and their API permits. Export, when it exists, is a
funeral rite: everything you had, flattened, after you've already
decided to leave. There's a quieter option, and it's older than the
database: a text file — if the text file had addresses."

**1:20 — What phext is.**
`[ON-SCREEN TEXT: "phext = plain text + coordinates"]`
`[SCREEN: a phext file open in a plain editor; scroll through raw text.]`
"Phext is plain text divided into chunks called scrolls. Each scroll
lives at a coordinate — nine numbers, grouped like a library address:
library, shelf, series; collection, volume, book; chapter, section,
scroll. Count the text inside a scroll as two more dimensions and you
get the tagline on the site: eleven dimensions, plain text, no
lock-in. A scroll that uses no structure is still just text — open it
anywhere. But because every scroll has an address, software can fetch
exactly one article, change exactly one article, and leave the other
twenty thousand untouched. And the structure costs almost nothing:
nine separator bytes, borrowed from the unused corners of the
character set, are the entire machinery. No binary format, no schema,
no container. If every phext tool vanished tomorrow, you could still
read the file with your eyes — the addresses are a convention written
in the text, not a lock on it."

**2:00 — Phext Edit: the tour.**
`[SCREEN: fresh browser tab → edit.phext.io. Let the page load on camera.]`
"This is Phext Edit, at edit.phext.io. It's the editor this format
ships with, it's open source, and the public utilities on the site run
without signing in — **[CONFIRM: which demo phext is offered to a
signed-out visitor today, and start there]** Start where any reader
starts: browsing. The phext opens as a space you can navigate —
dimension by dimension, scroll by scroll.
`[ON-SCREEN TEXT: "every scroll has an address"]`
Notice what's not happening: no page reloads from a database, no query
language. The file's own structure is the index."

**3:10 — Jump to a coordinate.**
`[SCREEN: navigate/jump by coordinate to a specific scroll; show the coordinate on screen.]`
"Because every scroll has an address, you can go straight to one.
Here's a coordinate — **[CONFIRM: use a coordinate that exists in the
demo phext on screen]** — and here is its scroll, immediately. That's
what a database index does, except the index isn't a separate machine
or a separate file. It's the shape of the text itself. An address you
can say out loud, write in a notebook, or put in a footnote — and it
still works in ten years, because it's just numbers in a text file."

**3:50 — Search.**
`[SCREEN: run a search for a word; results come back as coordinates.]`
"Of course you can search the whole lattice — and notice what a search
result *is* here: not a page, a coordinate. The place where the thing
lives. Search hands you addresses, and addresses are permanent in a
way URLs never were."

**4:30 — Edit one scroll, save.**
`[SCREEN: open one scroll, change one line, save.]`
"Now the edit. I change one line, in one scroll, and save. Phext
Edit holds edits in an overlay and rebuilds the file on save —
byte-perfect everywhere I didn't touch. One scroll changed; the rest
of the encyclopedia is bit-for-bit what it was. If you've ever watched
a wiki turn a one-line correction into an unreviewable database
transaction, you know why that matters: here, an edit is a diff a
human can read."

**5:15 — Download it, open it in Notepad.**
`[SCREEN: download/export the phext from Phext Edit — CONFIRM the affordance on the day; fallback below. Then open the .phext file in a mainstream plain editor (Notepad / VS Code class). Hold both views side by side: Phext Edit | raw editor.]`
"Now the lock-in test. Take the file out of the fancy editor and open
it in the most ordinary text editor on the machine. No phext tooling,
no plugin. Here's what you get: the text. All of it. Every article,
fully readable, in order — because a phext is plain text, and plain
text is the one format every computer since forever can open. What
you don't get is the structure rendered: the nine separator bytes
that carry the coordinates show up as odd little symbols or blank
boxes, and the articles run together separated by those marks. It's
not pretty. It's not supposed to be. Pretty is Phext Edit's job.
Survival is the file's job. Worst case — every phext tool gone, the
company gone, the website gone — you still have your encyclopedia,
readable and editable, in Notepad. That is what no lock-in actually
means."
