## 4. X package

**Announcement post** (≤280 chars — the link counts as 23):

> Wikipedia in a box. No database backend.
>
> phext = plain text where every article lives at a coordinate — one file you can diff, version, email, seal.
>
> New walkthrough: browsing, searching & editing it live in Phext Edit 👇
> edit.phext.io

**Thread (8 posts)**

1/ Every encyclopedia you've used is a database wearing a website as a
hat. The words are yours; the retrieval is rented. If the backend
dies, your knowledge is a backup file and a prayer.

2/ phext deletes the backend. Take plain text, divide it into scrolls,
give every scroll a coordinate — library.shelf.series /
collection.volume.book / chapter.section.scroll. The addressing IS
the index.

3/ A scroll with no structure is still just text: open it anywhere.
But with addresses, software can fetch exactly one article, edit
exactly one scroll, and leave the other 24,000 untouched. An edit
becomes a diff a human can read.

4/ This is Phext Edit — edit.phext.io. Browse the lattice, jump to a
coordinate, search and get addresses back, edit a scroll and save:
the file rebuilds byte-perfect everywhere you didn't touch.

5/ The lock-in test: download the phext and open it in Notepad. No
plugin, no phext tooling. You get all the text — every article,
readable — with little separator marks where the coordinates live.
Not pretty. Still yours, forever. That's the point.

6/ And when you want the box itself: our demo encyclopedia is 24
articles, 49 scrolls, 21,504 bytes — one file. One-coordinate edit
measured: 926 bytes before the scroll and 20,578 after it,
byte-identical. Sealed with xz: 9,300 bytes. Email-sized Wikipedia.

7/ Same format, production scale: the phcc compiler's entire source
rides in a phext of 64,399 scrolls. An encyclopedia is the same shape,
just more volumes. The addressing doesn't change as it grows.

8/ Try it: edit.phext.io — public demos in the browser, no sign-in
for the utilities, all open source. Write in 11 dimensions: plain
text, no lock-in. What would you put in the box?

