Title: Desktop SSO for Phext Notepad — the cookie paste is gone
Source: ~/workspace/phext-notepad-perf/REPORT-DESKTOP-SSO.md
Inscribed: 2026-11-08 07:47 CST
SHA-256: be4f829aa1c40d07db17e55a585c8e54e0885704dfb9972ba4ce4d24e02a8b9c

---
# Desktop SSO for Phext Notepad — the cookie paste is gone

Task 46.5.5/6.5.10/5.5.7 · 2026-11-08 · Status: **built, verified end to end,
both branches pushed. Merge + deploy + arming are Will's acts** (runbook at
the end).

Track B's sign-in worked but asked the user to paste the `phext_session`
cookie value out of the browser's developer tools once per 14 days. This
build replaces that with phext-edit's own SSO machinery, shaped for a
desktop app: the browser still does the provider sign-in, but it now
redirects back to the app itself, and the app trades what it catches for a
session — no paste, and no secret ever ships in the binary.

## A note on the base branch (read first)

The brief named lattice branch `emise/sso-exocortexia` @ 122ff68 as the
base. **That branch no longer exists on the remote — it was merged into
`exo` via PR #26** (merge commit eb450bc). Verified directly:
`git merge-base --is-ancestor 122ff68e33d7 origin/exo` is true, so exo
already carries the generalized allowlist this work composes with,
content identical to the named base plus the merge. The new branch is
therefore based on exo @ eb450bcd94230d6fe12ccaf9a01b5428421d1f8e rather
than on a deleted ref. Nothing was rebased; the branch is a single commit
directly on current exo.

## The final flow

1. **Probe.** Connect starts (after the stored-session reuse fails) with
   `GET /api/auth/sso/desktop?return=http://127.0.0.1:8912/&state=<probe>`
   and redirects off. **302 ⇒ armed** (desktop flow); **404 ⇒ unarmed or
   an older server** (paste fallback, shown only then).
2. **Catch.** The app binds a `LoopbackCatch`: a TCP listener on
   `127.0.0.1` at an ephemeral port, and mints a per-attempt state nonce
   (32 hex chars, SHA-256 over pid + wall clock + a process counter).
3. **Browser.** The app opens the system browser at
   `{server}/api/auth/sso/desktop?return={loopback URL}&state={nonce}`.
   - Signed out there: the server 302s to `/?next=<this endpoint,
     return+state preserved>` — the ordinary sign-in bounce — and after
     the provider sign-in the browser lands back on the mint.
   - Signed in: the server mints and 302s straight to the loopback
     return with `?assertion=<sealed>&state=<nonce>`.
4. **Land.** The catch ignores unrelated knocks (favicon, probes),
   requires the landing's `state` to equal the attempt's nonce, answers
   the browser with a small "you can close this tab" page, and hands the
   assertion to the app.
5. **Exchange.** The app POSTs `POST /api/auth/desktop/exchange` with
   headers `x-phext-assertion` / `x-phext-state` (the gate's route layer
   has no body channel; headers are how this API already carries the
   CSRF credential). The server verifies, burns, checks the editors
   list, and answers `200 {"session": <sealed phext_session>, "csrf":
   <token>}`.
6. **As before.** The client stores the returned pair in the same
   owner-only 0600 session file, verifies via `GET /api/session`, learns
   CSRF from it, and every Track B sync behavior proceeds unchanged
   (SyncBook base hashes, conflict model, one-open-phext refusal).

## The security argument

- **No secret ships.** A web consumer can hold `PHEXT_SSO_SECRET`; a
  distributed binary cannot — anything sealed inside it is public. So
  the desktop audience never verifies anything app-side: the app is a
  courier. The assertion is verified only by phext-edit, at the
  exchange, under the SSO secret that never leaves the server. The one
  server-side acceptance of an assertion is new and deliberate: the
  desktop audience is the exception to "this server never accepts one
  back," because the alternative (app-side verification) is the thing
  that cannot be done safely.
- **Single-use is enforced server-side.** Web audiences burned `jti`s
  themselves. The exchange burns the desktop `jti` into an in-memory
  set on the `Gate` (pruned by expiry on every burn, so it holds at
  most one 120-second lifetime's worth of exchanges) *before* the
  editor check: any verified presentation spends the assertion,
  including a non-editor's — there is no probing with a spent token,
  and replays answer the same flat 401 as every other failure (the
  endpoint never says which check a bad assertion failed).
- **Loopback validation is literal, not parsed.** `loopback_return`
  admits exactly: scheme `http` (no CA certifies loopback; RFC 8252
  native-app practice is plain http on the interface, which never
  leaves the machine), host the literal `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`
  (**`localhost` is rejected** — a name is whatever the resolver and
  the hosts file say it is, and the app binds the numeric loopback
  anyway; `127.0.0.2` and shorthand forms are rejected too), an
  explicit port 1–65535, and a plain-character path/query tail. No
  userinfo, no fragment, no whitespace or non-ASCII, total length
  capped so the value still fits the sign-in bounce's `next`. The
  check runs **before any session handling** at the mint (a bad return
  is a 400, never carried into the bounce) and again inside
  `sso_assertion_desktop` at the moment of sealing, so no caller can
  seal an unvalidated return.
- **Arming is a flag, not a URL.** Web audiences arm by configuring a
  target origin. The desktop audience has no fixed origin to configure
  — inventing one (e.g. a `PHEXT_SSO_DESKTOP_URL`) would be a lie about
  where assertions go. It is armed by `PHEXT_SSO_DESKTOP=1` alone (plus
  the existing real SSO secret). Until then, both desktop endpoints
  answer 404 — *before* any session handling, like every unconfigured
  audience — which is also exactly the signal the app's fallback keys
  on.
- **The state nonce, and the honest residual.** The app generates a
  fresh state per attempt; it travels in the mint request, rides
  **sealed inside the assertion** (new optional `st` claim —
  `skip_serializing_if` absent for web audiences, whose assertion bytes
  are unchanged), is echoed on the redirect, and the exchange demands
  it back and compares. The catch refuses a landing whose state is not
  its own attempt's, so an assertion minted for a different attempt —
  e.g. raced onto the listener by another local process — is detected,
  not exchanged. A wrong-state exchange attempt does *not* burn the
  assertion (it was never the attempt's exchange). Residual, stated
  plainly (RFC 8252 has the same one): a same-user local process that
  captures *both* the assertion and the state off the loopback redirect
  inside its 120 seconds can race the exchange — but that process is
  already inside the trust boundary the 0600 session file assumes, and
  what it could win is the same session the file holds.
- **Editor gate semantics mirror the web flow exactly.** The assertion
  mints for any signed-in account (`sso_admits`, unchanged). At the
  exchange, editors are checked **fresh against the current list** (not
  the assertion's `editor` claim). A non-editor receives
  `403 {"ok":false,"error":"This account is not on the editor list."}`
  — the gate's own refusal sentence — with no credential in the body,
  and the assertion is spent. The app's existing checks then also catch
  a signed-in non-editor via `/api/session` as before.
- **Session issuance is shared, not duplicated.** `Oauth::issue_session`
  is factored out of the OAuth callback's `finish()` (fresh CSRF +
  `SESSION_TTL` seal); the callback now calls it with unchanged
  behavior, and the exchange calls the same helper. Sessions handed to
  the app are ordinary sessions: the gate admits them, `/api/session`
  reads them, logout/revocation semantics are the server's as ever.

Routing note: the exchange takes its credential in headers because
`Gate::route()` — shared with `uml6d_edit` — receives no request body;
widening its signature for one endpoint was rejected. No changes to
`phext_edit.rs` or `uml6d_edit.rs` were needed at all.

## Files changed

**wbic16/phext-lattice — branch `emise/sso-desktop` @ 65cb909d** (1 commit
on exo eb450bc; +668/−12)

- `src/oauth.rs` — everything: `PHEXT_SSO_DESKTOP` arming
  (`Oauth::sso_desktop_ready`); `loopback_return` + `valid_state_nonce`;
  optional `st` claim on `SsoAssertionBody`; `Oauth::sso_assertion_desktop`;
  `Oauth::open_sso_assertion` (via an `open_with(secret)` refactor of
  `open`); `Oauth::issue_session` factored from `finish()`;
  `Gate::sso_desktop` mint handler (dispatched from `Gate::sso` ahead of
  the target lookup); `Gate::desktop_exchange` + the pruned in-memory
  burned-jti set; the exchange route arm. 10 new tests.
- `deploy/deploy.md` — new "Desktop (Phext Notepad) single sign-on"
  section in the style of the Exocortexia/Wishnode sections, plus a
  one-line pointer from the Wishnode section.

**wbic16/phext-notepad — branch `emise/desktop-sso` @ c83778ed** (1 commit
on `emise/edit-sync` a9f604e7; +603/−53)

- `native/core/src/sync.rs` — `EditClient::desktop_sso_url`,
  `desktop_sso_probe` (302/404 → `DesktopSso`), `desktop_exchange`
  (stores the returned session + CSRF on the client); `LoopbackCatch`
  (bind / state / wait / serve); `new_state_nonce`; form encode/decode.
  4 new tests. The module doc now describes the flow.
- `native/app/src/main.rs` — `sync_connect` probes the desktop endpoint
  when locked: armed → `desktop_sign_in` (catch on a thread, FLTK pumped
  while waiting, exchange, session verify); unavailable → the pre-
  existing paste block, moved verbatim into `paste_sign_in`. The shared
  tail (editor refusal, session store, library pick, pull) is untouched.
- `native/core/examples/sync_probe.rs` — new `desktop-login` command:
  probe, mock-browser bounce/mint/landing legs, exchange, session print,
  and `COOKIE`/`CSRF` lines for scripting.

## Tests

- **Lattice suite: 176 passed, 0 failed** (baseline 166 = 132 lib + 31
  phext-edit bin + 3 integration; now 142 lib + 31 + 3). The 10 new:
  loopback accept/reject table (incl. localhost, 127.0.0.2, https,
  missing/zero port, userinfo, fragment); desktop mint carrying state;
  signed-out bounce shape; bad return/state 400 before session work;
  404-until-armed for both endpoints; exchange happy path (session
  opens, CSRF matches, gate admits); burn/replay; wrong-state refused
  without burning; tampered/expired/foreign-aud/garbage all 401;
  non-editor 403 + spent.
- **Notepad core: 33 passed, 0 failed** (29 baseline + 4 new: URL
  encoding, nonce shape/distinctness, catch takes only its own
  attempt's landing, catch refuses a foreign attempt's landing). The
  FLTK app builds and links clean (needed the known VM workarounds:
  `TAR_OPTIONS=--no-same-owner` for fltk-sys, and X11 dev symlinks for
  libXinerama/libXcursor/libXfixes).
- **Joint E2E: 27/27 legs passed** against the **real `phext-edit`
  binary** built from `emise/sso-desktop` (three instances: armed+open
  editors, unarmed, armed+restrictive editors) and the **real
  `sync_probe`**. Harness: `~/workspace/desktop-sso/e2e/run_e2e.sh`
  (rerunnable; per-leg `LEG-PASS/FAIL` lines).
  - Desktop flow via the probe: probe-armed, signed-out bounce,
    mint redirect to the loopback, landing 200, exchange session
    identity (`github:424242`, editor), credentials yielded.
  - Round trip **on the exchanged session only**: open → update →
    save → fetch reads it back → **persisted in the on-disk phext** →
    pull (pulled=1) → local edit → push (pushed=1, saved=true) →
    fetch verifies the pushed text.
  - HTTP negatives (curl): mint, exchange 200, **replay 401, tamper
    401, wrong-state 401, localhost return 400, https return 400,
    unknown audience 404**.
  - Unarmed instance: mint 404, exchange 404, probe prints
    `PROBE unavailable` (the fallback signal the app keys on).
  - Non-editor instance: exchange **403** with the exact refusal text,
    **no `session` in the body**, second presentation **401** (spent).

### Which legs are harness-driven vs human-only

Harness-driven (all passing above): everything from the sign-in bounce
onward — mint, loopback, exchange, session use, sync round trip, and all
negatives — against unmodified server code from the branch.

Human-only, by necessity (same honest limit as Track B): **the provider
credential leg**. The local server's GitHub OAuth is configured with
dummy credentials, so a real GitHub sign-in cannot complete here; the
harness mints a `phext_session` cookie with the harness session secret,
sealed byte-for-byte as the server seals (verified: the server reads it
as `github:424242`, editor), and the mock browser presents it where a
signed-in browser would present its own. In production the browser's
cookie comes from Will's real sign-in; nothing downstream of that point
differs.

## Landing state

Both branches were pushed from aletheia-core over SSH as wbic16, each
parented on its stated base's fresh remote HEAD (verified on fresh
clones on the node immediately before pushing), force disabled, and
both refs re-verified through the GitHub API afterwards:

- `wbic16/phext-lattice` `emise/sso-desktop` = `65cb909dbfcc9dfb38d94e56e0f48da69e2dc3eb`
- `wbic16/phext-notepad` `emise/desktop-sso` = `c83778ed55129f43d91fcfa5ce622ca82508e6d1`

Merge-order note: `emise/drop-token-footer-cleanup` @ ca6495ce (token
removal + footer) is also based on exo eb450bc and does not touch the SSO
endpoints; `emise/sso-desktop` does not touch the token path or the shell
footer. The two branches are independent — either merge order works; any
overlap is confined to `src/oauth.rs` regions they do not share (the
drop-token work removes token plumbing; this work adds desktop arming,
handlers, and tests).

## Will's runbook

1. **Merge the lattice side.** Merge `emise/sso-exocortexia` is already
   in exo (PR #26) — nothing to do there. Merge `emise/sso-desktop`
   into exo, and `emise/drop-token-footer-cleanup` in whichever order
   you prefer (see the merge-order note).
2. **Deploy phext-edit** from the merged exo as usual
   (`deploy/deploy.sh` on the edit.phext.io host).
3. **Arm the audience.** Add one line to `/etc/phext-edit/oauth.env`:
   `PHEXT_SSO_DESKTOP=1` (`PHEXT_SSO_SECRET` is already there — no new
   secret) and restart phext-edit.
4. **Verify arming:**
   `curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' 'https://edit.phext.io/api/auth/sso/desktop?return=http://127.0.0.1:8912/&state=attempt-state-0123456789'`
   → **302** (toward `/?next=…` while logged out). 404 = the line or the
   restart didn't take; 400 = the check URL is malformed.
5. **Merge the notepad side.** Merge `emise/desktop-sso` (it sits on
   `emise/edit-sync`; that stack's merges are yours in whatever order
   you've planned) and use a notepad build from it. First Connect
   against the armed server runs the automated flow; against anything
   else it still offers the paste, so nothing strands if arming lags
   the app or vice versa.

## Open residuals

- **The provider leg is untested locally** (see above) — the first real
  sign-in on the deployed server is the true end-to-end test of the
  bounce with a live GitHub/Google/X session. All server-side legs
  after that point are covered.
- **Burned-jti state is per-process memory.** A phext-edit restart
  forgets spent jtis; a replayed assertion would still need to arrive
  inside its 120-second life *and* win the race against the restart —
  the same posture as the server's other in-memory auth state (rate
  limiter, pending sign-ins). If phext-edit ever runs multi-process,
  the burn set needs to move to shared state first.
- **The app's wait is 5 minutes** (catch deadline); a user who starts
  sign-in and wanders off gets a clean "sign-in timed out" and can
  retry. The listener dies with the attempt either way.
- **The exchange answers with the session in a JSON body** — by design,
  only over the authenticated exchange itself, never logged server-side
  (the handler logs nothing), and the app treats it exactly like the
  pasted cookie (0600 store). Anyone who preferred the cookie never
  exist outside a `Set-Cookie` header should weigh that this is a
  loopback-and-TLS API call the app itself initiates, equivalent in
  exposure to the paste it replaces.
- Windows/macOS builds of the notepad remain Will's to verify (the app
  change is platform-neutral Rust + the existing `open_url`; the catch
  binds `127.0.0.1`, which the server's validator admits on every OS).
